{"id":"df5491d9-851f-45b1-83c4-b7e1d7e2fc78","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025782,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    if nodes is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if required_symbols is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required_symbols')\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid node: missing required fields')\\n        node_map[node['id']] = node\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError('Parent node not found: ' + str(pid))\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if node['removable'] and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols:\\n            has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\\n            if not has_retained_child:\\n                retained.remove(nid)\\n                removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that, given a payload with nodes (list of dicts) and required_symbols (list), returns an object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nThe task describes \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" (Perses-style) but restricted to the given nodes. The instructions: consider deeper nodes before shallower; never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children; tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented; use node ID as tiebreak at equal depth; return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nWe need to parse the tree from nodes (each has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). The nodes form a tree. The goal: decide which removable nodes to remove such that all required_symbols are still present (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains) after removal? Actually \"required_symbols\" list; we need to preserve those symbols. Also root and nodes with retained children (descendants) cannot be removed? \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is retained, cannot remove it? That interacts with removal order: we process deeper nodes first, so children may be removed before parents; then the parent might become removable if all children removed? But \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained (not removed), cannot remove parent. So we must check after removal decisions for children.\n\nAlgorithm: Build a dict from node id to node object. Also build children mapping. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id (ascending? \"depth descending, then node ID as tiebreak at equal depth\" - should we use ascending ID? Typically for deterministic order, we sort by depth descending, then by id ascending. That ensures deeper nodes first, and stable tie.\n\nFor each node in that order (excluding root? Need to check if root removable? root removable = false, so okay), if node['removable'] is True, then we tentatively consider removing it. But we must check conditions:\n\n- The node's symbol is not in required_symbols? The instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, cannot remove it, even if removable? Probably yes. So skip if node['symbol'] in required_symbols. (But required_symbols likely includes \"root\" and \"s1\" in examples, so those nodes are not removable anyway.)\n\n- Check if any child of this node is retained (i.e., not yet removed). Since we process depth-first (deepest first), children are processed before parent. So we can maintain a set of retained_node_ids (initialized to all nodes). When we decide to remove a node, we remove it from retained set. We need to check if node has any child that is still in retained set. If yes, cannot remove.\n\n- Check if after removal, all required_symbols are still represented by at least one node that is retained (including the node itself if it's a required symbol? But we already can't remove required symbols). So we need to ensure that for each symbol in required_symbols, there is some retained node (not including this node if we remove it) that has that symbol. Since we only remove one node at a time, we can check if the node's symbol is in required_symbols and it's the only node with that symbol? Actually we already skip removing required symbols, so the only risk is removing a node that is not required but whose removal might cause a required symbol to disappear? Required symbols are symbol names, not node IDs. If we remove a node, does it affect required symbol presence? Only if that node was the only node with that symbol. The instruction: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means after removal, there must be at least one retained node for each required symbol. So we need to check, for each required_symbol, if there is some retained node (excluding current node if we remove it) with that symbol. For symbols other than the node's own symbol, it's fine. For the node's own symbol, if it's a required symbol (but we already skip removal of nodes with required symbols), so not applicable. So check: if the node's symbol is not required, then removing it won't affect required symbols. However, if the node is the only node with a particular required symbol (even though its own symbol is not that required symbol?), no, only the node's own symbol matters. So actually the check is simpler: we skip nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols. Then no required symbol will be removed. But what about a node that is not required symbol but its removal might cause a required symbol to become missing because that required symbol only appears in its descendants? No, required symbols are symbols of nodes; if a node has a required symbol, it must be retained. Removing its ancestors doesn't affect its presence. So only direct removal of a node with required symbol matters. So we can simply skip any node whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nBut also root can't be removed (removable false anyway). \"required symbol\" refers to required_symbols list, which includes \"root\" and \"s1\" in examples. So we skip those.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build dict of nodes by id.\n- Build children list per node.\n- Initialize retained = set of all node ids.\n- Determine list of all nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending (string comparison? ID like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. For tiebreak, stable but we can use the id string itself as sort key; since IDs are unique, it's fine).\n- For each node in that order:\n  - if node['removable'] is True and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols:\n    - check if any child of node is in retained: if yes, skip (cannot remove because has retained children).\n    - else, we can remove it: remove node['id'] from retained, and add to removed list.\n- After loop, removed_node_ids is list in removal order (the order we processed, which is depth descending then id). retained_node_ids is sorted? The output specification requires \"retained_node_ids\" as an array, \"removed_node_ids\" as array. The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically? Or by ID?). \"sorted retained IDs\" likely means lexicographic order (since IDs are strings). For removal order, it's the order we removed them. So we need to maintain removal list in the order we decided to remove. That matches the processing order.\n\nWe also need to consider nodes that are not removable (removable false) – they are always retained. No need to check.\n\nAdditionally, the instruction says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" We already sort depth descending.\n\nBut note: the parent-child relationship: we have parent field (string). For root, parent is null. For others, parent is id of parent. We need to compute children mapping.\n\nAlso, the case may be infeasible? If required symbols cannot be satisfied? But given nodes, they already represent required symbols. If we cannot remove any node due to constraints, that's fine. Output retained = all nodes, removed = [].\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) and return the JSON object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nEdge cases: The nodes list may be empty? Possibly but not in fixtures.\n\nWe need to raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. What constitutes structurally invalid? E.g., missing fields, invalid parent references, cycles? But we can trust the payload is valid per fixture. However, we should validate: check that each node has 'id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility'. If missing, raise ValueError. Also check that root exists and parent of root is None. Also check that for each node, parent is either None or exists in nodes. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso check that required_symbols is a list of strings. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nBut to keep it simple, we can assume valid payload per fixture, but it's good practice to validate required fields.\n\nOutput: sorted retained IDs? We need to sort them. Which order? Perhaps lexicographic order of id strings. Since IDs are like \"root\", \"n1\", etc., but \"root\" comes before \"n1\" lexicographically. That seems fine.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build dict\n    node_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\n            raise ValueError(\"Missing fields in node\")\n        node_map[node['id']] = node\n    # Build children map\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            if pid not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(\"Parent not found\")\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if node['removable'] and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols:\n            # Check if any child retained\n            has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\n            if not has_retained_child:\n                # Remove\n                retained.remove(nid)\n                removed_order.append(nid)\n    # retained set now contains retained node IDs\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)  # sorted alphabetically\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nTest on example fixtures. In the first fixture, nodes: n2 removable true, n4 removable true, n6 removable true. Others false. Required symbols: root, s1. So nodes with symbol s1: n1 (removable false), n4 (removable true). n4 has symbol s1, so cannot remove because symbol in required_symbols? Actually \"required_symbols\" includes \"s1\". So n4's symbol is \"s1\", so it is a required symbol node. So we cannot remove n4. Also n1 is not removable. n2 symbol \"s2\" not required, removable true. n6 symbol \"s0\" not required, removable true. Depth: n2 depth 1, n4 depth 2, n6 depth 2. Process deepest first: both n4 and n6 depth 2. Sort by depth descending (-depth), then id ascending. -2 for both, compare ids: \"n4\" vs \"n6\" => \"n4\" < \"n6\", so n4 first. n4: removable true, but symbol \"s1\" is in required_symbols, so skip. Next n6: removable true, symbol \"s0\" not required. Check children: n6 has no children (since no node has parent \"n6\"? In fixture, n6 parent is n1, so n6 has no children in children mapping). So no retained child, so remove n6. retained becomes minus n6. Then n2 depth 1: removable true, symbol \"s2\" not required. Check children of n2: n4 is child (parent n2). n4 is still retained (since we didn't remove n4). So has retained child -> cannot remove. So only n6 removed. So removed_order = [\"n6\"]. retained_sorted = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually root is there, plus n1,n2,n3,n4,n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Lexicographic: \"n1\" < \"n2\" < \"n3\" < \"n4\" < \"n5\" < \"root\"? Actually \"root\" comes after \"n5\"? Compare: 'n' vs 'r' => 'n' < 'r', so \"n5\" < \"root\". So sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. That seems plausible.\n\nBut check if we need to include node n6? No, removed. So output matches expected? Not given, but plausible.\n\nSecond fixture similar, n4 symbol s1 (required), so n4 not removable. n6 removable. So similar result.\n\nThird fixture same pattern.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nBut note: the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Since we skip nodes with required symbols, that condition is automatically satisfied. However, what if a required symbol appears only on nodes that are removable? But we skip removal of those nodes anyway. So no issue.\n\nAlso, \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" We check children retained.\n\nEdge: root has removable=false, so not considered.\n\nNow, about sorting retained IDs: The instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". That likely means ascending lexicographic order. We'll do that.\n\nNow, we need to produce the JSON with the implementation.\n\nThe output must be exactly the JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being Python source code (string). We need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the function as a string with proper indentation.\n\nWe'll also include validation code as described.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3303,"total_tokens":5475,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2926},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}